Have the client send an `Idempotency-Key` header per logical order attempt, and make the insert conflict-safe on `(account_id, key)`:

```js
router.post("/", async (req, res) => {
  const key = req.get("Idempotency-Key");
  if (!key) return res.status(400).json({ error: "Idempotency-Key header required" });

  const row = await db.write(
    `INSERT INTO orders (account_id, sku, quantity, idempotency_key)
     VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4)
     ON CONFLICT (account_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING
     RETURNING *`,
    [req.user.accountId, req.body.sku, req.body.quantity, key]
  );

  if (row) return res.status(201).json(row);

  const [existing] = await db.query(
    "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE account_id = $1 AND idempotency_key = $2",
    [req.user.accountId, key]
  );
  res.status(200).json(existing);
});
```

Needs a schema change: add an `idempotency_key` column to `orders` and a unique index on `(account_id, idempotency_key)` — scoped per account, not global, so two accounts can reuse the same client-generated key.

Fork worth naming: this stores the key on the `orders` row itself, which is simplest since only this endpoint needs it. If idempotency later spreads to other write endpoints, a separate `idempotency_keys(account_id, key, response, created_at)` table that caches the full response is the more general shape — not needed yet here.

Left out: this treats a replayed key as "same request" and returns the original row unconditionally, even if `sku`/`quantity` differ from the first call. A stricter version would compare the payload and 409 on mismatch — worth deciding, not implementing by default.

